    "summary": "Kente cloth, a rectangular man&#039;s garment, hand-woven in blue and white imported cotton, composed of 28 hand-woven narrow strips hand-sewn together along the selvedges. The 28 strips are made up of eight different patterns, which repeat regularly three times, and then irregularly for the last four strips. Five of the patterns are purely warp-faced; the other three have modifications introduced by variations in the weft. The warp ends have been hemmed by hand sewing.",
